## #5 Logging items, one by one

**5a. _Default retention 90 days vs the 6-month rule**
- ⚠️ New recurring cost: as far as I know, retention beyond 30 days is billed per GiB-month. Check Cloud Logging pricing.
- You probably can't edit the bucket, so this likely goes to an admin.
- Console: Logging → Logs Storage → `_Default` → Edit → Retention **180** days.
- Check:
```bash
gcloud logging buckets describe _Default --location=global --project=$P --format="value(retentionDays)"
```

**5b. VPC flow logs off**
- ⚠️ New recurring cost: flow logs volume scales with traffic. Set sampling to 0.5 to start.
- It changes the shared `default` subnet in me-central2, so it likely needs an admin.
- Console: VPC network → `default` → me-central2 subnet → Edit → Flow logs **On**.
- Check:
```bash
gcloud compute networks subnets describe default --region=me-central2 --project=$P --format="yaml(enableFlowLogs,logConfig)"
```

**5c. Data Access audit state unknown**
- You can't read the IAM policy, so ask an admin to enable DATA_READ and DATA_WRITE for Secret Manager, Cloud Storage and Cloud SQL (Console: IAM & Admin → Audit Logs).
- ⚠️ Cost: Secret Manager read logs fire on every app start.
- Check whether any exist today:
```bash
gcloud logging read 'logName:"cloudaudit.googleapis.com%2Fdata_access"' --project=$P --freshness=7d --limit=3 --format="value(timestamp,protoPayload.serviceName)"
```

**5d. DDDDDD console.log printing the key**
- Dev team removes the `console.log`. If the key ever reached logs, rotate it, because logs hold it for 90 days.
- Check 7 days (your earlier check covered 24h):
```bash
gcloud logging read 'logName:"backend_pm2_out" AND (textPayload:"DDDDDD" OR jsonPayload.message:"DDDDDD")' --project=$P --freshness=7d --limit=5 --format="value(timestamp)"
```

**5e. Request bodies logged ("Body : {...}")**
- Bodies can carry passwords or personal data, and they sit in Cloud Logging for 90 days.
- Dev team removes the body logging or redacts sensitive fields.
- Count only, no content:
```bash
gcloud logging read 'logName:"backend_pm2_out" AND jsonPayload.message:"Body :"' --project=$P --freshness=1d --limit=1000 --format="value(timestamp)" | wc -l
```

**5f. SECRET_KEY "abc" in next.config.ts**
- Ask the dev team if it is a placeholder.
- As far as I know, values in Next.js's `env` block are inlined into the browser JavaScript at build time. A real key there would be public, so it belongs in a server-side secret instead.

